A||||17||87PegasusGT||NOS question||||1||
Z||000000||87PegasusGT||03-09-2000||03:41 PM||||Ok. So if a 50hp shot is fine for high mileage and 70hp for low, what can I get away with on a 3.4? I was told if I go 3.4TDC, aluminum heads won't like more than 50hp.Is this true? I should be able to go 70-100 hp with the Camaro 3.4... right? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||03-09-2000||04:42 PM||||The 3.4 block is stronger than the 2.8/3.1 blocks, but you're still limited by the cast pistons (in my opinion). Sounds like the 70 shot will work ok if you use some common sense, that seems to be a good match with the 60 degree V6 engines. I'm not sure I'd go higher without forged pistons. Of course there will be someone out there that will claim he had a 250 shot system on a stock 2.8 and it works just fine...<I>sure</I>!||||reg||1
Z||000003||87PegasusGT||03-09-2000||06:31 PM||||Do you think it would cost more to do TDC swap, or to do the standard 3.4 and build it to match? Does the 3.4 TDC use stronger pistons? Will the aluminum heads have a problem with warping under NOS?||||reg||1
Z||000004||olympic||03-09-2000||08:25 PM||||The 3.4 TDC is a very strong engine with forged crank, connectiong rods, pistons and sodium filled exhaust valves.  The engine is good past 8000 rpm but is limited to 7200 rpm to protect the a/c and other accessories.It's weakness is the timing belts(or is it chains??) and tensioners which need to be changed often.   It will cost more for the TDC swap than a regular 3.4 swap.  The TDC will probably handle nitrous better though.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Smokey||03-09-2000||10:44 PM||||hey buddy, If ya wanna put nitrous on your car.....u could just wheel it on over to my house and I'll just beat the hell out of the engine with my trusty sledge hammer and save ya the money||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||03-09-2000||11:19 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>Can anyone tell me if I can put a blower, a turbo, and nitrous on the 3.4 TDC.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||gdogg||03-09-2000||11:33 PM||||You could take the engine out all together and put a jet engine in. That would be cool.||||reg||6
Z||000008||Phaeton||03-10-2000||01:12 AM||||I don't know.  Batboy, if you have the turbo feeding the blower, and nitrous kicking in on demand, shouldn't there be a bottle of clean burning propane to spray in simultaneous with the nitrous? and wouldn't this make the 3.4 a little too much?  A duke would be more appropriate, "2M4" the logo would read.||||reg||1
Z||000009||1FST2M6||03-10-2000||01:32 PM||||Not sure if you guys are familiar with the tuning firm HKS or CNM Well HKS makes a kit for the (toyota 4agze) To "twin charge" it. it uses an electronicly actuated bypass valve and at 3800rpm the stock supercharger will stop pressureizing the intake and let the turbo take over the s/c boosts to 14psi and the turbo already making boost will then LAUNCH the car from 1bar (14.7psi) to about 1.5bar or (21psi). The kit runs about $2700 plus install. CNM makes almost the same kit for the Starion and Conquest. Taking the tiny 2.4 liter 4 banger and force feeding it an additional 500+hp the technology has been around since HKS started makeing the Toyota kit back in 1984 for the Corolla and MR2 mk1. As for the Nitrous. you'll have no problem puting 70hp on just about any motor as long as you have enough gas to support the boost in both intake pressure and speed of combustion. I'm technicly only runing a 40 hp shot but its nearly 80lbft of torque! putting my numbers at the flywheel at 210hp and 310lbft. (add 27.5% to these #s  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6dyno.jpg" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6dyno.jpg</A>  I've gone throught 16!!! bottles now and about 5000 miles and my car still has the same compression as it did before i had the nitrous, sill burns NO oil, leaks no-where and will lift the front tires off the ground at will.   VIVA LA N2O   Oh.. i'm selling my nitrous kit too.. Already ordered a 9psi  supercharger, will be here any day... I'll make sure and keep everyone posted on my results.||||reg||1
Z||000010||87PegasusGT||03-10-2000||01:34 PM||||Smokey: I realise you don't like instant hp, but it is the single most power per $ add on you can get. As long as you are going to say fake, I should probably forget turbos, supercharging, racing fuel, Dual Cams, extra cylinder valves, and fuel injection and just go get an outdated carburated big block. But for those of us with money concerns, please let us work off of our budgets in peace. I doubt anyone with money to blow would go buy NOS if they could afford other options.<BR>   Back to my question. The TDC can take more abuse? I was told that you can't really build it like you can a standard motor because of the aluminum heads. Anyone had experience modifying them?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||1FST2M6||03-10-2000||01:42 PM||||Just a quick question.. not trying to stur up anything to muddy.. But why is everyone so  ANTI-nitrous? I've had no problems with my car on the bottle, other than traction problems, there are about 10 other cars in my area (all come to the same shop to both have the bottles filled and to be tuned) and none of us have had any problems. I have had ONE major malfunction due to nitrous but it was MY own fault not the nitrous' fault. Why is everone so affraid of the shiney blue bottle? Cause their friends, brothers', girlfriends dad blew up his V8 Citation with nitrous? Just curious...||||reg||1
Z||000012||Spyder||03-10-2000||02:26 PM||||I like nitrous oxide.  It is a bargain for the amount of horsepower gained.<BR>When I get my car truly road worthy, I will probably add some nitrous.<P>I’m working on a project:<BR>When you turn the engine off, the doors automatically lock and windows roll up and a few shoots of N20 fills the passenger compartment. Just kidding, would be funny though. (more ways than one)<p>[This message has been edited by Spyder (edited 03-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||BBBAD GT||03-10-2000||05:53 PM||||I'm with 1FST2M6, if used properly, it shouldn't really do any damage to your motor.  Note I said "properly", and not stupidly.  I've seen one too many fiberglass hoods blow 20 ft in the air in the burn box for guys who mistakenly hit the n2o during their burnout...neat to watch/hear.  I've used it, and liked it for what it was worth.<P>I love the misconception that people have that the N2O is flamable (not that I'm saying anybody here has).  "Don't smoke around that bottle".  Its the fuel that mixes with the n2o that is flamable (duh).  <P>Just my thoughts.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||olympic||03-10-2000||07:58 PM||||1FST2M6,<BR>   I remember an article in HOTROD a number of years back.  It featured a Chevy Eurosport(Lumina) powered by a 454 with three blowers!!!  It had one big blower with another smaller one on each side feeding it. It looked awesome and they said the guy would drive it on the street regularely(there was even a baby seat mounted between the wheel tubs in the back)   They asked him why on earth would you want to put 3 blowers on a car?  His reply was..."cause there wasn't room for 5!"  LOL||||reg||1
